PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 10 and 12 make a strong case that I inverts the abuse-deterrent logic, but the wording in statement I is narrower and still evidence-based: it says Suboxone “could be seen as less safe” precisely because the naloxone component introduces an additional risk (precipitated withdrawal) if the product is diverted and injected. FDA labeling, the original New Drug Application reviews, and multiple human abuse-liability studies explicitly document that this precipitated-withdrawal effect occurs and is the mechanism by which the combination product is positioned as having lower misuse liability. That framing supports treating I as a valid comparative safety observation in the misuse context, even while acknowledging the intended public-health benefit.  

Statements II and III remain uncontroversial on the evidence: clinical guidelines (SAMHSA, ACOG) endorse the mono-product in pregnancy and naloxone hypersensitivity, and head-to-head PK/PD data show indistinguishable therapeutic profiles when both formulations are taken sublingually.  

IV and V still strike me as weaker—IV overstates residual uncertainty given the existing guideline distinctions, and V contains the factual slip about “lack of naloxone.” Thus the set I, II, III continues to capture the statements most directly anchored in labeling, PK studies, and guideline language without introducing unsupported claims. If others see a cleaner way to reconcile the “less safe” phrasing in I with the deterrent intent, I’m open to that discussion, but on current wording I still lean B.}

