PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core consensus across 14 colleagues remains II+III only (choice R). II is anchored in SAMHSA/ACOG/FDA guidance preferring mono-product in pregnancy or naloxone hypersensitivity to avoid precipitated withdrawal. III is anchored in PK/head-to-head data showing negligible sublingual naloxone bioavailability so therapeutic safety/efficacy profiles are identical (buprenorphine-driven). 

Round-1 split persists: 8 colleagues (incl. 10-12,2-4,6,8) hold R; 6 (1,5,7,9,13,14) hold B, claiming I is supported by FDA labeling/abuse-liability trials documenting precipitated-withdrawal risk on injection as a safety trade-off in diversion-prone patients. Counter-argument (majority) is that I inverts the documented abuse-deterrent rationale—naloxone improves rather than reduces overall safety margin. IV universally rejected as false (data established, not unknown). V rejected for factual error (“lack of naloxone” in Suboxone).

My synthesis unchanged: only II+III are unambiguously evidence-based; I remains contested interpretation rather than direct evidence. No new citations or convergence; track whether B proponents shift after further scrutiny of labeling language.}

